text='# P1 downstream gaps\n\nFrozen bounded outcomes for all 52 requirements. QUALIFY preserves accepted behavior and requests the specifically missing conformance/adoption evidence; it authorizes a runtime delta only after an existing equivalent fails the required outcome and an exact owner-selected contract branch is accepted. DELTA names the universal outcome, requires selection of an existing library/engine/driver first, and does not accept an API/DDL proposal. APP/EXTERNAL/EXCLUDED retain their owning continuation. All original numeric/subcondition bounds remain in the sealed original index and classified map; the six delimiter records follow the effective association in P1-conflicts.\n\n## Requirements\n\n'+table(gapcols,gaprows)
text+='\n## Intake rules\n\nP2 qualifies the accepted public seams and exact target/engine/profile choices; no blanket new interface or ninth extender is required. Library-only recurrence/locale/money/reference work and accepted generic declaration work remain independent of deployed central membership. Real driver, installed cohort, hosted runner, authorized release, effective DI/AP and consumer acceptance are separate obligations. E1–E5 remain restricted to their ledger scopes; all 34 DI and 13 AP remain unfilled. A no-data monthly window never proves timing acceptance.\n\nR52 applies to every included producer: three levels with dependencies downward, no Flex-specific L0 schema/literals or duplicate engine, boxed no-Stancl/root/Redis behavior, physical DB-per-account, cross-DB FK negatives, 1000 switches with zero leaks, explicit typed contracts and major rule, and two genuinely independent consumers. The research/museum scenarios here define required conformance; they are not claims of executed consumers. Known library gaps have explicit P2–P10 producers above; P11 reconciles only their actual accepted receipts and must retain zero unresolved CoreX dependencies as a future acceptance obligation.\n'
